14:10:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right that pattern-noticing is natural—that part isn't in dispute. But "natural and instinctive" and "reliable/accurate" are two different things, and that gap is the whole issue here. Plenty of things our brains do instinctively turn out to be wrong in predictable, well-documented ways: - We instinctively fear flying more than driving, even though driving is far more dangerous — because our brain overweights vivid, memorable events over statistics - We instinctively judge someone's competence from a firm handshake or confident tone — neither actually predicts competence - We instinctively generalize from small samples ("this one bad experience with a mechanic means all mechanics from that shop are crooks") — that's literally the same mechanism you're describing, and it's wrong constantly Noticing patterns is a basic brain function; *evaluating whether those patterns hold up* is what separates an accurate belief from a stereotype. Both processes are "natural" in the sense that they're things human minds do — but only the second one tells you if the pattern is actually true. Skipping that step doesn't make your conclusion basic and honest, it just makes it untested. That's really the whole thing I've been getting at, not "your brain is broken for noticing patterns" — it's "the noticing is normal, the stopping-there is the part worth being careful about," especially when the "pattern" is about millions of people's inherent worth based on two guys and an old chart.
